# Google Sheets synchronization — outbox + append-only raw tab (rev 2)
# Paste into https://play.d2lang.com/
# Suggested layout engine: elk
#
# REV 2: the IT sheet is ordered NEWEST-FIRST (new tickets inserted at the top).
#        Top-insertion shifts every existing row down by one, which invalidates
#        any stored row index. Storage is therefore separated from presentation.

direction: right

title: |md
  # Google Sheets Sync — rev 2 (newest-first display, append-only storage)
| {near: top-center; shape: text; style.font-size: 26}

app: Application API {
  shape: rectangle
  style.fill: "#1f6feb"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"
}

db: PostgreSQL {
  shape: cylinder
  style.fill: "#4c8dfa"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"

  tickets: tickets
  outbox: "sync_outbox\n(row_key, raw_row_number)"
}

q: Redis Queue {
  shape: queue
  style.fill: "#4c8dfa"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"
}

worker: Sync Worker {
  style.fill: "#1f6feb"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"

  claim: "1. Claim batch\nSELECT ... WHERE status='PENDING'\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED"
  map: "2. Project to sheet row\nemployee_id → name\nassigned_to → technician name\nmain_issue_id → label"
  locate: "3. Locate row in _raw\nraw_row_number if cached,\nelse find row_key (ticket_no)\nin column B"
  push: "4. New → values.append to _raw\n Existing → values.update at\n cached row (rows never shift)"
  mark: "5. Mark SENT +\ncache raw_row_number"
  retry: "On failure: attempts++,\nexponential backoff,\nstatus=FAILED after 5 tries"

  claim -> map -> locate -> push -> mark
  push -> retry: "429 / 5xx"
  retry -> claim: "requeue"
}

gcp: Google Cloud Platform {
  style.fill: "#8b949e"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"

  sa: "Service Account\n(JSON key or Workload Identity)"
  api: "Sheets API v4"
}

sheet: Google Sheet — IT Tracker {
  style.fill: "#8b949e"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"

  raw: "_raw (hidden, protected)\nAPPEND-ONLY — row N never moves\nDate | Ticket No | Employee | Dept |\nMain Issue | Concern | Assigned To |\nStatus | Remarks" {
    shape: page
  }

  view: "Tickets (what the IT team sees)\n=QUERY(_raw!A2:I,\n \"select * where A is not null\n order by A desc, B desc\", 0)\n→ newest at the top, as today" {
    shape: page
  }

  raw -> view: "recalculates automatically"
}

dlq: Ops Alert / Dead-letter view {
  shape: hexagon
  style.fill: "#d1242f"
  style.font-color: "#ffffff"
}

app -> db.tickets: "write ticket"
app -> db.outbox: "write outbox row\n(same transaction)"
app -> q: "dispatch job\n(after commit)"
q -> worker.claim: "consume"
worker.claim -> db.outbox: "read PENDING"
worker.map -> db.tickets: "join for names"
worker.push -> gcp.api: "HTTPS"
gcp.sa -> gcp.api: "OAuth 2.0 access token"
worker.push -> gcp.sa: "sign JWT"
gcp.api -> sheet.raw: "append / update"
worker.mark -> db.outbox: "status = SENT"
worker.retry -> dlq: "after max attempts"

rules: |md
  **Why the raw tab exists**

  The IT team's sheet shows newest-first, meaning each new ticket is
  inserted at the top and **every row below shifts down by one**. A stored
  row index is therefore stale the moment the next ticket is encoded — a
  retry or a status update would silently write to the wrong ticket's row.
  That is data corruption, not a cosmetic bug.

  Splitting the sheet fixes it at the root: the worker only ever *appends*
  to `_raw`, so row N is row N forever, and the `Tickets` tab reproduces the
  newest-first order the team already expects via a QUERY formula. Nobody
  has to change how they read the sheet.

  **Trade-off to raise with the IT team before building this:** a QUERY
  output range is computed, so the `Tickets` tab becomes read-only. If they
  currently type remarks straight into the sheet, that workflow moves into
  the app. This is arguably a feature — it is what "the database is the
  system of record" actually means in practice — but it is a real change to
  their day and should not arrive as a surprise.

  **If they must keep hand-editing that tab**, the fallback is to insert at
  index 1 with `InsertDimensionRequest` and pin identity to each row with
  Sheets **developer metadata**, which survives row shifts. More API calls,
  more moving parts, same correctness. Do not fall back to storing a bare
  row index.

  **Other rules (unchanged)**

  - **One-way sync only** (DB → Sheet). Bidirectional editing creates write
    conflicts the SRS has no rule to resolve. Protect `_raw` so only the
    service account can write it.
  - **Idempotency** — `row_key` (the ticket_no) identifies the row, so a
    retried job updates rather than duplicating.
  - **Rate limits** — ~60 write requests/minute/user. Batch 100 rows into one
    call, especially during the historical backfill.
  - **Denormalization happens at the boundary**, not in the DB. IDs stay in
    Postgres; names are resolved only when projecting the sheet row.
  - **Google Cloud Console setup**: enable the Sheets API → create a service
    account → download the JSON key → share the spreadsheet with the service
    account's email as Editor.
| {near: bottom-center; shape: text}
